 ## Snapshot Import
 
 Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
+## Index Build
+
+On successful validation, build a new SQLite database in a staging location from the snapshot's newline-delimited JSON records. Create a table `equipment` with columns `serial TEXT PRIMARY KEY`, `calibration_date TEXT`, `due_date TEXT`, `description TEXT`, and `snapshot_date TEXT`. Create an index on `serial`. Populate the table in a single transaction. On commit success, atomically replace the active database file and update the stored snapshot date metadata. The previous database remains available if the build fails.
+
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. Lookup is performed against the local SQLite index with an indexed `serial` column to meet the 250 ms target on 4 GB RAM kiosks.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; Managed search service | Predictable lookups with no service to run; each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival. Keeps all registry concerns inside the registry component and satisfies the no-network tenet. |
+| Snapshot activation | Staged build + atomic replace | In-place update | Ensures a failed import leaves the last valid snapshot available and avoids exposing partially imported records. |
